WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:07.060
Cell clues and feeding showed that a mushroom is not a plant.

2
00:00:08.320 --> 00:00:10.800
They cannot show who its closest relatives are.

3
00:00:11.960 --> 00:00:14.120
How could the cells' own instructions tell you?

4
00:00:15.100 --> 00:00:18.320
The first film climbed three rungs of the evidence ladder.

5
00:00:19.620 --> 00:00:22.960
Now climb the top rung, DNA, with the same six cards.

6
00:00:24.033 --> 00:00:28.793
The order of the chemical letters in a stretch of DNA is its DNA sequence.

7
00:00:30.173 --> 00:00:32.593
Each protein is built from a gene's instructions.

8
00:00:33.953 --> 00:00:37.253
So comparing proteins is another way to compare genes.

9
00:00:38.367 --> 00:00:41.907
A protein is a chain of building blocks called amino acids.

10
00:00:43.227 --> 00:00:46.467
To compare, line up the same protein from two living things.

11
00:00:47.787 --> 00:00:49.527
Each place in the line is a position.

12
00:00:50.887 --> 00:00:54.107
At each position, the building blocks match, or they do not.

13
00:00:55.150 --> 00:00:58.830
Here is a made-up example, only ten building blocks long.

14
00:01:00.230 --> 00:01:03.650
Line up living thing A with living thing B, and count the matches.

15
00:01:05.050 --> 00:01:06.270
Then line up A with C.

16
00:01:07.350 --> 00:01:10.310
A matches B more closely in this one protein.

17
00:01:11.570 --> 00:01:15.570
That is one clue that A and B may share a more recent common ancestor.

18
00:01:16.830 --> 00:01:19.630
That is an organism both groups descend from.

19
00:01:20.633 --> 00:01:22.893
But a match count is a clue, not proof.

20
00:01:24.213 --> 00:01:27.253
One group's version can change faster than another's.

21
00:01:28.093 --> 00:01:32.753
So scientists build family trees from many genes before they trust a grouping.

22
00:01:33.933 --> 00:01:40.113
In 1993, Sandra Baldauf and Jeffrey Palmer looked at 25 proteins from animals,

23
00:01:40.613 --> 00:01:43.133
fungi, plants and other living things.

24
00:01:44.433 --> 00:01:46.673
One protein is called EF-1 alpha.

25
00:01:47.783 --> 00:01:53.163
In this protein, the animals and fungi they studied share an extra stretch, 12

26
00:01:53.163 --> 00:01:54.283
building blocks long.

27
00:01:55.603 --> 00:01:56.743
Do the plants have it too?

28
00:01:58.063 --> 00:01:59.743
Decide what each answer would mean.

29
00:02:06.217 --> 00:02:07.977
The plants did not have it.

30
00:02:08.997 --> 00:02:14.277
The simplest story is one change, in an ancestor that animals and fungi share but

31
00:02:14.277 --> 00:02:20.637
plants do not. And all four proteins they used to draw family trees grouped animals

32
00:02:20.637 --> 00:02:21.637
with fungi.

33
00:02:22.367 --> 00:02:24.347
So the evidence supports this tree.

34
00:02:25.767 --> 00:02:28.307
Fungi and animals sit on neighbouring branches.

35
00:02:29.747 --> 00:02:30.827
Plants sit farther off.

36
00:02:32.187 --> 00:02:37.047
Fungi and animals share a more recent common ancestor than either shares with

37
00:02:37.047 --> 00:02:40.147
plants. Many other proteins agree.

38
00:02:41.167 --> 00:02:45.987
One last warning. A closer relative is not the same kind.

39
00:02:47.447 --> 00:02:51.307
Fungi are closer to animals, but a mushroom is still not an animal.

40
00:02:52.687 --> 00:02:54.927
Closer means a more recent common ancestor.
